---
import Presentation from "../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `led1.value(1)
time.sleep(1)
led2.value(1)
time.sleep(1)
led3.value(1)
time.sleep(1)`;
const Figure2 = `def turn_on_and_wait(led):
    led.value(1)
    time.sleep(1)`;
const Figure3 = `turn_on_and_wait(led1)
turn_on_and_wait(led2)
turn_on_and_wait(led3)`;
const Task1 = `from machine import Pin
import time

led1 = Pin(0, Pin.OUT)
led2 = Pin(1, Pin.OUT)
led3 = Pin(2, Pin.OUT)

def turn_on_and_wait(led):
    led.value(1)
    time.sleep(1)
`;
const Task2 = `from machine import Pin
import time

led1 = Pin(0, Pin.OUT)
led2 = Pin(1, Pin.OUT)
led3 = Pin(2, Pin.OUT)

def turn_on_and_wait(led):
    led.value(1)
    time.sleep(1)

turn_on_and_wait(led1)
turn_on_and_wait(led2)
turn_on_and_wait(led3)
`;
const Task3 = `from machine import Pin
import time

led1 = Pin(0, Pin.OUT)
led2 = Pin(1, Pin.OUT)
led3 = Pin(2, Pin.OUT)

def turn_on_and_wait(led, wait):
    led.value(1)
    time.sleep(wait)

turn_on_and_wait(led1, 1)
turn_on_and_wait(led2, 0.7)
turn_on_and_wait(led3, 0.4)
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.5: Writing Your Own Functions</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.5</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Writing Your Own Functions</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Functions</p>
              <p>def</p>
              <p>Parameters</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Look Back at Yesterday</h2>
              <p>
                Activity 2.4's countdown turned on three LEDs, one at a
                time, with the exact same two lines repeated for each.
              </p>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>What's a Function?</h2>
              <p>
                A <b class="text-sn-theme">function</b> is a named,
                reusable block of code &mdash; write the steps once, then
                run them again just by using the name.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Defining a Function</h2>
              <p>
                <code>def</code> starts a function definition. The word
                inside the parentheses, <code>led</code>, is a
                <b class="text-sn-theme">parameter</b> &mdash; a
                placeholder for whatever gets passed in later.
              </p>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Calling the Function</h2>
              <p>
                Each call passes in a different LED for the
                <code>led</code> parameter &mdash; same two lines of
                behavior, three different pins.
              </p>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Identical behavior to yesterday. Far less repetition.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Defining a function with <code>def</code></li>
                <li>Using a parameter to make a function work with different values</li>
                <li>Replacing repeated code with function calls</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Function</dt>
                  <dd>A named, reusable block of code.</dd>
                  <dt>def</dt>
                  <dd>The keyword that starts a function definition.</dd>
                  <dt>Parameter</dt>
                  <dd>A placeholder variable in a function definition, filled in with a real value each time the function is called.</dd>
                  <dt>Call</dt>
                  <dd>Actually running a function by writing its name followed by parentheses.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Using the same three-LED circuit from Activity 2.4, create a
            new MicroPython script named <b class="text-sn-theme">2-5-functions.py</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Define the Function</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Set up the same three LEDs as Activity 2.4.</li>
            <li>Define a function <code>turn_on_and_wait(led)</code> that turns the given LED on, then waits one second.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-5-functions.py" mark="8-10" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Call It Three Times</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Call <code>turn_on_and_wait()</code> once for each LED, recreating yesterday's countdown with far less repeated code.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-5-functions.py" mark="12-14" ins="12-14" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Add a Second Parameter</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add a <code>wait</code> parameter to the function so each call can use a different pause length.</li>
            <li>Make the countdown accelerate: 1 second, then 0.7, then 0.4.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-5-functions.py" mark="8-9,12-14" ins="8-9,12-14" />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>When you run your script:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>All three LEDs should turn on in sequence, just like Activity 2.4</li>
            <li>After adding the <code>wait</code> parameter, each LED should turn on a little faster than the one before it</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>What repetition did defining <code>turn_on_and_wait()</code> eliminate from your Activity 2.4 code?</li>
            <li>What does the parameter <code>led</code> represent inside the function definition, and how does it get a real value each time you call the function?</li>
            <li>Why might adding a second parameter like <code>wait</code> be useful beyond just this countdown?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
